import type { Prose } from '../full';

const PROSE: Record<string, Prose> = {"caesars-will-44bce":{"body":"Days after the Ides, Caesar's will was unsealed and read at Mark Antony's house. It passed over Antony — consul, Caesar's cavalry commander, the obvious heir — and named as principal heir Gaius Octavius, Caesar's great-nephew: eighteen years old, sickly, unknown, and away across the Adriatic at Apollonia with the army assembling for Parthia. The will adopted him as Caesar's son, gave him three-quarters of an enormous estate, left Caesar's gardens beyond the Tiber to the Roman people and 300 sesterces to every citizen — and, in a detail nobody missed, listed Decimus Brutus, one of the assassins, among the secondary heirs. Antony, who controlled Caesar's papers and treasure, declined to release the money.\n\nRoman testamentary adoption made the young man Gaius Julius Caesar, and the name, not the estate, was the inheritance that counted. Against the advice of his stepfather, who told him to refuse a legacy that would get him killed, Octavius crossed to Italy, took the name, and began paying the dead dictator's bequests himself — out of loans, auctions of his own property, and money that may have been Caesar's war chest. Veterans and the urban plebs noticed which of the two claimants was honoring Caesar's promises and which was sitting on the funds.\n\nNearly everyone misread him. Antony sneered at a boy who owed everything to a name; Cicero planned to use him against Antony and then discard him — the young man was to be praised, honored, and removed, he wrote to a friend, punning in Latin on the last word's second meaning. Both were dead within four years, and the pun was on them.","whyItMatters":"The will converted a private estate into a political weapon, because in post-Marian Rome the things that actually conferred power — soldiers' loyalty, the plebs' affection, a network of colonies and clients — were personal property, and personal property can be bequeathed. The Republic had no office to inherit; it turned out not to need one. Legions would defect to the name Caesar for the next decade and a half, and the constitutional settlements to come would be built by the name's owner. The civil wars of the next fourteen years are, at bottom, a probate dispute settled with legions.","sources":[{"author":"Suetonius","work":"The Deified Augustus","note":"Written ~150 years later with archival access; hostile anecdote and court tradition sit side by side."},{"author":"Nicolaus of Damascus","work":"Life of Augustus","note":"A courtier's panegyric written under Augustus — the authorized version of the loyal heir's story."},{"author":"Appian","work":"The Civil Wars"}],"misconception":{"claim":"Caesar's will made Octavian his political successor.","correction":"A will could transfer property and a name, not office — the dictatorship was not heritable and the Republic had no throne. What Octavian inherited was money and a claim, and converting the claim into power took fourteen years of civil war. That the claim proved sufficient shows how personal Roman power had already become."}},"mutina-43bce":{"body":"By early 43 the Senate, driven by Cicero's Philippics, had declared war in all but name on Antony, who was besieging Decimus Brutus — one of Caesar's assassins — at Mutina to wrest Cisalpine Gaul from him. Both consuls, Hirtius and Pansa, marched north, and the Senate legalized Octavian's private army of Caesarian veterans by granting the nineteen-year-old propraetorian command. In two battles that April Antony was beaten and driven over the Alps — and both consuls were dead, Hirtius in the fighting, Pansa of his wounds. Octavian stood in northern Italy in effective command of eight legions, the only army the Republic had.\n\nThe Senate then made every available mistake: honors and the supreme command for Decimus, the assassin of Octavian's adoptive father; the promised bounties for Octavian's soldiers trimmed; the boy himself pointedly excluded. He sent four hundred centurions to Rome to request the consulship for him. When the Senate refused, one centurion showed it the hilt of his sword: this would do it, if the senators would not. Octavian marched, took the city without a blow, and was elected consul on 19 August at nineteen, with his cousin Quintus Pedius. A law of Pedius condemned Caesar's assassins in absentia — and then the new consul went north again, not to fight Antony but to bargain with him.","whyItMatters":"The Senate armed Octavian on the theory that legitimacy was the Senate's to grant and withdraw; Mutina disproved the theory inside four months. Cicero's plan to praise, honor, and remove the boy assumed the armies belonged to the state, but they belonged to Caesar's memory, and the boy carried the name. Sulla had marched on Rome with a grievance; Octavian marched with an inheritance, and the soldiers — not the voters, not the senators — decided which claims to office were real. Every step of the next decade follows from that demonstration.","sources":[{"author":"Cicero","work":"Philippics","note":"Contemporary and utterly partisan — the monstrous Antony of the tradition is substantially Cicero's construction, and it shortly cost him his head."},{"author":"Appian","work":"The Civil Wars"}]},"second-triumvirate-43bce":{"body":"In November 43 Antony, Octavian, and Lepidus met for two days on a river island near Bononia, their legions watching from opposite banks, and carved up the Roman world. On 27 November the tribune Publius Titius carried a law creating them tresviri rei publicae constituendae — three men for setting the state in order — a supreme magistracy for five years, with power to legislate without Senate or assemblies, appoint magistrates years in advance, and judge without appeal. The First Triumvirate had been a private handshake with no legal existence; this one was the constitution, amended in due form to suspend itself.\n\nTheir first act was the proscriptions. Lists went up in the Forum: some 300 senators and 2,000 equestrians, their lives forfeit, their property confiscated, rewards for killers and informers — including slaves informing on masters. The motive was money as much as vengeance: forty-three legions needed pay, and confiscation looked like revenue until the auctions flooded the market and prices collapsed. The shortfall produced new taxes, including one on 1,400 rich women, which produced in turn the extraordinary spectacle of Hortensia arguing the women's case in the Forum — successfully. Many of the proscribed escaped to Sextus Pompey's Sicily; Appian preserves pages of escapes, betrayals, and wives who saved or sold their husbands.\n\nThe most famous name on the lists was Cicero, killed on 7 December as his litter was carried toward the coast. His head and right hand — the hand that wrote the Philippics — were nailed to the Rostra he had spoken from. Later tradition made Antony the butcher of the proscriptions and minimized Octavian's part; the sorting of blame was done by historians writing under the man who won.","whyItMatters":"The legality is the lesson. Sulla's proscriptions had at least been a dictator's improvisation; the triumvirs procured a statute, moved by a tribune, voted by an assembly, in proper form. Every republican institution operated correctly, once, to abolish republican government — proof that constitutional forms do not defend themselves when the incentives beneath them have inverted. It also means Octavian's power is never again technically illegal: from the lex Titia forward he always holds a lawful office, which is exactly what will make his eventual claim to have restored the Republic so difficult to refute and so hollow.","sources":[{"author":"Appian","work":"The Civil Wars","note":"The fullest account of the proscriptions, written two centuries later; the vivid escape narratives may owe as much to literature as to record."},{"author":"Plutarch","work":"Life of Cicero"},{"author":"Ronald Syme","work":"The Roman Revolution","note":"Reads the triumvirate as a party seizing the state — an analysis shaped by watching the dictatorships of the 1930s, and itself an interpretation."}],"misconception":{"claim":"The triumvirates were backroom alliances between three powerful men.","correction":"That describes only the First Triumvirate, which was a private bargain with no legal standing. The Second was a formal magistracy created by law, with near-absolute power for five years. The difference is the point: by 43 BCE the machinery of the Republic could be operated, in due form, to end the Republic."}},"philippi-42bce":{"body":"In autumn 42 the war reached the Via Egnatia. Brutus and Cassius had spent two years squeezing the eastern provinces — Rhodes sacked, the Lycian city of Xanthus destroyed itself resisting — and brought perhaps nineteen legions to a fortified position at Philippi in Macedonia; Antony and Octavian brought as many against them, perhaps 200,000 men in all, the largest battle Romans ever fought against Romans. Two engagements three weeks apart decided it. In the first, Brutus's wing broke through and plundered Octavian's camp — Octavian himself was ill and absent, hiding in the marshes, warned (his own memoirs claimed) by a friend's dream — while Antony stormed Cassius's lines. Cassius, wrongly convinced the day was wholly lost, had his freedman kill him. In the second battle, on 23 October, Brutus's officers forced a fight he did not want; his army broke, and he fell on his sword.\n\nThe aftermath sorted the two victors. Antony laid his scarlet cloak over Brutus's body. Octavian — so the hostile tradition says — sent the head to Rome to be thrown at Caesar's statue, and told prisoners begging for burial that the carrion birds would see to it. He had won nothing on the field and the soldiers knew it. The division of spoils gave Antony the rich East, with Parthia to avenge and tribute to raise; Octavian got Italy — and with it the assignment everyone dreaded, confiscating land in eighteen designated Italian towns to settle tens of thousands of discharged veterans.\n\nOne artifact catches the cause's contradiction. Brutus paid his troops with a silver denarius showing daggers and the cap of liberty — and, on the other face, his own portrait, the living-ruler's vanity that had damned Caesar. The republican army fought for pay and land like everyone else's; its coinage had to advertise both tyrannicide and the paymaster.","whyItMatters":"Philippi ended the possibility that a Roman army would fight for the Republic as an idea: both sides' legions were Caesarian in formation and mercenary in motive, and once the tyrannicides' cause died with its cash, no force anywhere fought for the old constitution again. The division of spoils then set the geometry of the final war — and it favored the apparent loser. Antony took the wealth; Octavian took the odium of confiscation in Italy, and with it the land, the veterans, the recruiting grounds, and the stage on which Roman legitimacy was performed. The hated job turned out to be the prize.","sources":[{"author":"Appian","work":"The Civil Wars"},{"author":"Plutarch","work":"Life of Brutus","note":"The tradition canonized Brutus as philosophy's martyr; the nobility of the death scene is literature as much as record."},{"author":"Suetonius","work":"The Deified Augustus","note":"Source of the post-battle cruelty stories, drawn from a pamphlet tradition hostile to Octavian — they may be true, invented, or both."}],"misconception":{"claim":"Augustus was a great general who conquered his way to power.","correction":"Octavian's battlefield record was poor — at Philippi he was ill, absent, and lost his camp; Antony won the actual fighting, and Octavian's later wars were won by Agrippa. His genius was political: picking lieutenants, choosing moments, and controlling the story afterward."}},"perusine-brundisium-41bce":{"body":"Settling the Philippi veterans meant evicting Italian smallholders by the town-full, and the evicted had champions: Antony's brother Lucius, consul for 41, and Antony's wife Fulvia, who between them raised an army — partly for the dispossessed, mostly against Octavian's grip on Italy. Octavian and Agrippa penned Lucius inside the old Etruscan hill town of Perusia through the winter. Archaeology preserves the siege's tone: lead sling bullets survive inscribed with obscene messages addressed to Fulvia and to Octavian's backside. The town starved, surrendered in February 40, and burned; Lucius was spared and shipped off to a Spanish command, while Fulvia fled east and died. A darker tradition — three hundred captured senators and equestrians slaughtered at an altar of the Deified Julius on the Ides of March — was doubted even in antiquity, but its existence shows what people believed Octavian capable of.\n\nWhen Antony landed at Brundisium that autumn, the confrontation fizzled: the legions on both shores refused to fight each other, and negotiators built the Treaty of Brundisium instead. The world was split at Scodra in Illyricum — Antony the East, Octavian the West, Lepidus tolerated in Africa — and the bargain was sealed by marrying the newly widowed Antony to Octavian's sister Octavia. Poets hailed a new golden age. It lasted about as long as such things do.","whyItMatters":"Brundisium shows where sovereignty had migrated: the triumvirs wanted a war and their own soldiers vetoed it, because Caesarian veterans would not kill Caesarian veterans while pay and pensions flowed. And Perusia shows why the veto held — Italy's hatred of the confiscations could not become resistance, because the only armed men in Italy were the confiscations' beneficiaries. Octavian absorbed the odium and kept the asset. When the last war came, he would hold Italy's manpower, its oaths, and its grievances, all at once.","sources":[{"author":"Appian","work":"The Civil Wars"},{"author":"Suetonius","work":"The Deified Augustus","note":"Preserves the altar-sacrifice story with visible hesitation; atrocity tales attached themselves to Octavian's early career and were never fully sorted from fact."}]},"sextus-pompey-39bce":{"body":"Sextus Pompeius, the younger son of Pompey the Great and a survivor of Munda, had assembled a state out of the Republic's wreckage: a great fleet based on Sicily, manned by his father's clients, republican die-hards, refugees from the proscriptions, and escaped slaves, holding Sardinia and Corsica besides. Commanding the western sea lanes, he cut the grain shipments, and Rome went hungry; when new taxes came on top of hunger, rioters stoned Octavian in the Forum. In 39 the triumvirs were forced to negotiate. The Pact of Misenum confirmed Sextus in Sicily, Sardinia, Corsica, and the Peloponnese, promised him a consulship, and let the surviving proscribed come home. At the celebratory dinner aboard his flagship, his admiral Menodorus whispered an offer to cut the anchor cables and make him master of the world; Sextus answered that Menodorus should have done it without asking.\n\nThe peace barely lasted a year. Octavian's propaganda painted Sextus as a pirate chief commanding a slave rabble; strip the slur and there remains the last non-Caesarian power in the West — the heir to Pompey's name, clientele, and naval instincts — whose fleets wrecked Octavian's first two attempts against him, with help from the weather, and kept Rome's bread supply hostage for most of a decade.","whyItMatters":"The blockade taught the coming regime its most durable lesson: whoever feeds Rome rules Rome. Octavian's later fixations — Egypt annexed and sealed off as a personal possession, the grain supply run by his own equestrian prefect — descend from the hungry years when a Pompey with a fleet could set bread prices inside the capital. The war also created Agrippa's navy and made sea power a Roman state priority for the first time since Carthage; the fleet that would win at Actium was built to beat Sextus.","sources":[{"author":"Appian","work":"The Civil Wars","note":"Transmits Octavian's framing of Sextus as a pirate running slaves; the label did its work then and still distorts the record now."},{"author":"Cassius Dio","work":"Roman History"}]},"virgil-program-38bce":{"body":"While the triumvirs fought, Octavian's friend Maecenas — an Etruscan-descended knight who never held office and never needed to — began collecting poets. Virgil, whose family land near Mantua sat inside or dangerously near the post-Philippi confiscation zone, published the Eclogues around 39–38 and entered the circle; through Virgil came Horace, who had fought for Brutus at Philippi as a young officer and come home to a confiscated farm and a clerk's desk. Maecenas gave Horace a Sabine estate and Virgil protection, and what he took in exchange was never crude dictation. It was patronage — the oldest Roman social bond, patron and client, extended to literature at the moment literature would decide what the wars had meant.\n\nThe poems carry the era inside them. The first Eclogue is two herdsmen: one saved by a godlike young man in Rome, the other driven off his land into exile — the confiscations seen from both ends in a single conversation. The grief is real, and so is the gratitude, and the tension between them never resolves. From this circle, over the next two decades, would come the Georgics and the Aeneid: the regime that emerged from the wars would be the first in Roman history with a curated literature.","whyItMatters":"Octavian grasped earlier than any rival that a regime resting on ambiguous constitutional forms needs poets more than a dynasty needs genealogists: what law could not say — that the new order was ancient, fated, and pious — epic could. The Aeneid would do for the Principate what no statute managed, giving one family's ascendancy the feel of destiny. The deeper consequence is ours: when the modern world remembers Rome, it largely remembers the version this workshop built, which is why the era's propaganda problem is not a footnote to its history but a property of the sources.","sources":[{"author":"Virgil","work":"Eclogues","note":"Eclogue 1 stages the confiscations directly — the program and its human cost held in one poem."},{"author":"Ronald Syme","work":"The Roman Revolution","note":"Reads the poets as instruments in an organization of opinion; most Virgil scholars find more independence — and more grief — in the verse than Syme allowed."}]},"naulochus-36bce":{"body":"Octavian could not beat Sextus Pompey at sea; Agrippa could. Recalled from Gaul, he spent 37 cutting a training harbor — Portus Julius — through the necks of land joining Lake Avernus and the Lucrine lagoon to the sea, building heavier ships, and drilling crews to use the harpax, a catapult-fired grappling hook of his own devising. On 3 September 36, off Naulochus on Sicily's north coast, some 300 ships a side met, and the grapples turned a sailing battle into infantry fighting on decks, which Romans always won. Sextus escaped east with seventeen ships and was executed in Asia the next year by one of Antony's officers.\n\nThen came the coda. Lepidus, landed in Sicily with fourteen legions for the campaign, took the surrender of Sextus's land forces, claimed the island, and ordered Octavian out. Octavian instead walked into Lepidus's camp with a small escort and the name Caesar; the legions came over to the name, and Lepidus was left begging for his life in a borrowed cloak. He was stripped of the triumvirate, his provinces, and his armies, kept only the office of pontifex maximus, and lived out three more decades in comfortable, guarded irrelevance at Circeii.","whyItMatters":"One month demonstrated two facts about the new politics. Competence could be delegated: Octavian never needed to become a general, because Agrippa won the wars and handed over the credit — a partnership no rival matched, least of all Antony, who had no Agrippa. And armies had become pure Caesar-property: Lepidus held an island, twenty-plus legions, and full legal equality, and lost all of it in an afternoon to an unarmed man with the right name. The triumvirate was now a duumvirate, and the last war had found its two sides.","sources":[{"author":"Appian","work":"The Civil Wars"},{"author":"Velleius Paterculus","work":"Roman History","note":"A loyalist officer-historian: his Octavian enters Lepidus's camp as a hero. It may equally have been the gamble of a man out of options."}]},"antony-parthia-36bce":{"body":"The campaign was meant to avenge Carrhae and crown Antony as the soldier of the age. He took one of the largest armies Rome had ever fielded in the East — sixteen legions with cavalry and allied contingents, perhaps 100,000 men — and, avoiding the Mesopotamian plain that had killed Crassus, swung north through Armenia toward Media. Then he outran his own siege train to move faster; the Parthians caught the train and destroyed it along with its two escorting legions. Without siege engines, the assault on the Median capital Phraaspa stalled; the Armenian king Artavasdes read the omens and went home; and the retreat, begun in early winter, became the disaster — twenty-seven days through the mountains, eighteen running battles, thirst, dysentery, and snow. Perhaps 24,000 legionaries died, most of them not in combat. Cleopatra met the survivors on the Syrian coast with money and clothing.\n\nAntony's dispatches announced a success. Rome, hearing otherwise, ran the comparison: the same season, Octavian and Agrippa had destroyed Sextus Pompey and disarmed Lepidus.","whyItMatters":"The failure did more than kill men; it inverted the partners' trajectories in a single year. Antony's primacy had always rested on being the proven commander — after 36 that capital was spent, while Octavian's ledger showed Naulochus. Worse, rebuilding a shattered army without begging Italian recruits from his rival made Antony structurally dependent on the one treasury available to him: Cleopatra's. Everything that follows — the Donations, the marriage politics, the ease with which the alliance was painted as un-Roman — walks through the door this dependence opened. Parthia, as at Carrhae, is the fact Roman ambition breaks on.","sources":[{"author":"Plutarch","work":"Life of Antony","note":"The retreat narrative is powerful and tragic by design; the casualty figures cannot be checked."},{"author":"Cassius Dio","work":"Roman History"}]},"donations-34bce":{"body":"In 34, after a face-saving campaign in which he captured the Armenian king who had abandoned him — parading him through Alexandria in chains, in what Rome heard described as a triumph celebrated outside Rome — Antony staged a ceremony in the city's gymnasium. He and Cleopatra sat on golden thrones, she dressed as Isis; Cleopatra was proclaimed Queen of Kings, ruling Egypt and Cyprus jointly with her son Caesarion — declared, in the same breath, the legitimate son of Julius Caesar. Alexander Helios, her elder son by Antony, received Armenia, Media, and Parthia (which no one had conquered); the infant Ptolemy Philadelphus got Syria and Cilicia; Cleopatra Selene got Cyrenaica. Much of this distributed territory that Rome governed as provinces, held as client kingdoms, or did not hold at all.\n\nHow much of the scene is real is genuinely hard to say. Our accounts descend from Octavian's war propaganda, which needed exactly this picture: a Roman commander dissolving into an eastern court and giving Roman lands to a foreign queen's children. Antony's own defense, in letters and through allies, treated the arrangements as ordinary client-kingdom administration — the tool Rome had used in the East since Pompey — dressed in the ceremonial the region expected. Both things can be true; titles were cheap, and most of the thrones were paper.\n\nThe Caesarion declaration was not paper. Octavian's entire position rested on being Caesar's son by adoption; a recognized son of Caesar's body, co-ruler of the richest kingdom in the Mediterranean, was a standing counter-claim to the only asset Octavian could not share.","whyItMatters":"The Donations mattered less for what they changed in the East — client-king reshuffles were routine — than for what they threatened in Rome. Against a blood son of Caesar backed by Egypt's wealth and Rome's greatest living general, Octavian's adoptive claim was suddenly the weaker Caesarism, which is why the propaganda war now escalated toward annihilation and why, in 30, Caesarion could not be allowed to live. The ceremony also handed Octavian a sellable war: after two decades of Roman killing Roman, he could finally frame the coming fight as Rome against a foreign queen — the framing that would carry Actium.","sources":[{"author":"Plutarch","work":"Life of Antony","note":"Written ~130 years later from sources shaped by Octavian's wartime propaganda."},{"author":"Cassius Dio","work":"Roman History"},{"author":"Adrian Goldsworthy","work":"Antony and Cleopatra","note":"Argues much of the ceremony was conventional client-kingdom administration, inflated by hostile reporting into an eastern coronation."}],"misconception":{"claim":"Cleopatra was an Egyptian queen.","correction":"By descent she was Macedonian Greek — the last of the Ptolemies, the dynasty planted by one of Alexander's generals three centuries earlier. She was reportedly the first of her line even to learn the Egyptian language, and her self-presentation as Isis was statecraft aimed at her subjects, not ancestry."}},"will-seized-32bce":{"body":"In 32 the triumvirate's legal term had lapsed, and legitimacy was up for grabs: both consuls and some 300 senators left Rome to join Antony, who now formally divorced Octavia. Then two of Antony's senior men defected the other way and told Octavian where Antony's will was deposited — with the Vestal Virgins, who refused to surrender it. Octavian took it by force, an act of plain sacrilege, and read selections to the Senate: Caesarion affirmed as Caesar's true son, vast legacies to Cleopatra's children, and instructions that Antony be buried beside Cleopatra in Alexandria. Whether the document was genuine, doctored, or honestly read is unknowable — doubts were raised even in antiquity — but the burial clause did its work: Rome could now believe Antony had stopped being Roman.\n\nWhat followed was choreography. Italy and the western provinces swore a personal oath of loyalty to Octavian — of its own free will, his memoir insisted. War was declared with the revived fetial rite, a spear hurled into symbolically enemy ground before the temple of Bellona — against Cleopatra only. Antony was stripped of his consulship-designate, and not named an enemy at all.","whyItMatters":"The declaration's shape is the masterpiece. A third round of Roman-on-Roman war in one generation was unsellable, so Octavian sold a national crusade against an eastern queen instead — every war-weary Italian could join the winning side without killing fellow citizens, and Antony's supporters could defect without technically committing treason. Octavian got the civil war he needed while never legally fighting one, and the oath of 32 gave his extra-constitutional position a personal, Italy-wide foundation that outlasted the war. The technique — refounding domestic power on a foreign emergency — has been studied ever since.","sources":[{"author":"Cassius Dio","work":"Roman History","note":"Reports the will's contents as Octavian published them; no independent text ever existed, and forgery or selective reading cannot be excluded."},{"author":"Plutarch","work":"Life of Antony"},{"author":"Augustus","work":"Res Gestae","note":"The winner's own memorial: all Italy swore the oath 'of its own free will.' The phrase protests a little too much."}]},"actium-31bce":{"body":"On 2 September 31 BCE, at the mouth of the Ambracian Gulf in western Greece, Antony's fleet tried to break out of a blockade that had been strangling it all summer. Cleopatra's squadron ran the gap with the war treasury and made for Egypt; Antony followed with a fraction of his ships; the rest turned back and surrendered, and his abandoned land army — nineteen legions — gave up within days without a battle. As fighting, Actium was modest and one-sided. As a political fact it was total: the last force that could oppose Octavian dissolved, Egypt fell within a year, and Antony and Cleopatra were dead. Rome would remember it as the foundation-battle of a new age — and nearly everything we know about it comes from the winner.\n\nThe battle was lost before it was fought. In the spring Agrippa had seized Antony's supply stations along the Greek coast while Octavian took position north of the gulf, and through the summer Antony's forces sat bottled in a malarial anchorage: hunger, fever, and a steady bleed of desertions — client kings first, then Romans, including his most senior consular, who rowed to the enemy dying of fever (Antony sent his baggage after him), and the officer Dellius, who defected carrying the battle plans. By September, Antony had burned the ships he could no longer crew.\n\nOf the action itself we have only the outline, and the numbers are disputed. Antony's remaining fleet of heavier ships came out in line; Agrippa's lighter, more numerous vessels stretched them; in the afternoon, when the sea breeze rose, Cleopatra's sixty ships under sail cut through the center and ran south — and sails, stowed by every fleet before battle, mean flight was planned. Antony transferred from his flagship to a smaller vessel and followed with a few dozen ships. The famous version — the besotted general chasing his panicking queen and abandoning his men — is the Augustan version. The sober reading is a breakout attempted from a lost position, which partly succeeded: the treasury escaped, everything else was forfeit.\n\nThen the real construction began. Octavian founded a Victory City, Nicopolis, on the site of his camp, with games; a monument above it displayed the bronze rams of captured warships — its empty sockets, still measurable, are the propaganda program's physical residue. Virgil set the battle on the shield of Aeneas: Augustus with the Senate, the people, and the great gods of Rome on one side; barbaric wealth, an Egyptian consort, and barking Anubis on the other. Horace's ode on Cleopatra calls her a fatal monster — then grants her a death so noble it undercuts the slur. A civil war between two Roman warlords was repainted, within a decade, as the salvation of the West from the East.\n\nThat is why this event's reliability chip reads contested. Not the outcome — Octavian won, utterly — but the texture: the scale of the fighting, the intentions of the losers, Cleopatra's role, all reach us through accounts that descend from the victor's memoirs and the literature he sponsored.","whyItMatters":"Actium matters twice. As an event, it ended the cycle of civil war that had run, with pauses, since Sulla — not by settling any constitutional question but by leaving exactly one man alive with an army, which turned out to be the only settlement that held. As a story, it was the new regime's founding myth: Octavian needed his victory to be a refounding rather than a faction's win, so Actium was built into temples, games, a city, and the best poetry in the Latin language, while Antony was written into caricature. The construction worked for twenty centuries — the drunk Antony, the scheming queen, the destined Augustus are all still the reflex version. No event in the corpus shows more clearly that the winner takes not just the spoils but the archive.","sources":[{"author":"Plutarch","work":"Life of Antony","note":"Partly family memory — his great-grandfather was requisitioned to haul grain for Antony's fleet — but the narrative descends from Augustan-era accounts."},{"author":"Cassius Dio","work":"Roman History","note":"The fullest battle narrative, written 250 years later, complete with invented speeches."},{"author":"Adrian Goldsworthy","work":"Antony and Cleopatra","note":"Reads Actium as a breakout from a campaign already lost — the battle as anticlimax, the myth as the real event."}],"misconception":{"claim":"Actium was a titanic naval battle that Antony lost when Cleopatra panicked and fled, and he abandoned his men to chase her.","correction":"It was a breakout from a lost position by a starving, deserting force, and Cleopatra's departure with the treasury was almost certainly the plan — her squadron carried sails into battle, which fleets expecting to stand and fight did not. The panicked-flight story is Augustan propaganda that turned a strategic escape into a morality play."}},"deaths-30bce":{"body":"The final campaign was barely a campaign. Octavian advanced through Syria in the summer of 30 while the East changed sides ahead of him — Herod of Judaea, an Antony man for a decade, switched allegiance in person and kept his throne. On 1 August Alexandria fell; Antony's remaining fleet and cavalry went over without fighting. On a false report of Cleopatra's death, Antony fell on his sword, then was carried, dying, to the fortified mausoleum where she had barricaded herself with her treasure, and was hauled up through a window to die in her arms. Nine days later, after failed negotiations with Octavian — who wanted her alive to walk in his triumph — Cleopatra died in a manner uncertain even to contemporaries. She was thirty-nine, had ruled for twenty-one years, and was buried beside Antony, as his will had asked.\n\nThe children were sorted by threat. Caesarion, seventeen, had been sent up the Nile toward the Red Sea and India; his tutor persuaded him to turn back on Octavian's promises, and he was killed. One of Octavian's advisers supplied the justification with a Homeric pun: no good thing, a multitude of Caesars. Antyllus, Antony's eldest son by Fulvia and the only other adult heir, was dragged from a shrine and beheaded. The younger children of Antony and Cleopatra were spared, walked in the triumph, and were raised in Rome — by Octavia. Cleopatra Selene ended her days a queen in Mauretania.\n\nEgypt itself passed to the conqueror, on terms Rome had never seen — the next era opens with them. In 29 the doors of the temple of Janus, open in war, were closed for the first time in two centuries.","whyItMatters":"The killings define the new order's logic before the order has a name. After Actium, Octavian pardoned lavishly — clemency was cheap once resistance was impossible — but Caesarion and Antyllus died, because the regime's one non-negotiable asset was exclusive title to the name Caesar. That is monarchy's arithmetic, performed by a man still calling himself a magistrate of a republic: rivals in policy can be forgiven; rivals in blood can only be removed. The succession obsession that will shadow the Principate for four centuries — and the violence of nearly every imperial transition — is already visible, complete, in the strangling of a seventeen-year-old.","sources":[{"author":"Plutarch","work":"Life of Antony","note":"The death scenes are the most novelistic pages in ancient biography — Shakespeare lifted them nearly whole."},{"author":"Cassius Dio","work":"Roman History"}],"misconception":{"claim":"Cleopatra killed herself with an asp smuggled past the guards in a basket of figs.","correction":"The snake is one ancient version among several. Plutarch, our fullest source, already reports alternatives — poison hidden in a hollow hairpin — and notes that no snake was found in the chamber. Her death was uncertain within living memory; the asp won because it made the best image."}}};
